1. **U: confirmed.** [Section 8a, lines 132–142](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:132).  
   After clearing denominators, distinct integer objective values differ by at least one. Multiplication by \(B=2^n\) makes that difference dominate the entire binary tie-breaking code, whose range is \(0,\ldots,B-1\). Consequently, \(q\) has exactly one minimizer whenever \(F\) is nonempty, and it minimizes the original objective. The verifier guesses only \(x\); membership checking and the exact inverse zero test are deterministic. Every inverse call therefore has a verified feasible target, and exactly one guess accepts an attainable threshold. Polynomial encoding length is preserved.

   Thus threshold and nonemptiness belong to UP. NP-hardness **under polynomial many-one reductions** implies NP=UP. For everywhere-nonempty classes with recognizable validity, the same unique optimum certifies the complementary threshold, giving UP∩coUP and the additional NP=coNP consequence. None of these statements establishes forward-P.

2. **O: confirmed as an oracle lower bound.** [Section 8b, lines 146–150](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:146).  
   The specified inverse oracle reveals precisely membership. An entirely negative transcript remains consistent with emptiness and every unqueried singleton. Distinguishing emptiness therefore requires \(2^n\) queries in the deterministic worst case. Under the nonempty-singleton promise, \(2^n-1\) negative queries suffice to infer the last candidate, and fewer leave two indistinguishable possibilities.

   The explicit-input/NPO limitation is correctly stated. This proves neither NP-hardness of an explicitly represented family nor a counterexample to the book’s converse.

3. **B: confirmed.** [Section 8c, lines 154–169](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:154).  
   When \(X_0\not\subseteq A\), its 0/1 bottleneck value is one. A strict improvement exists exactly when a feasible set lies entirely inside \(A\). Every verification query retains the same feasible target and original instance.

   Threshold binary search is valid because the containment predicate is monotone. Greedy pruning preserves existence; each failed deletion remains impossible after subsequent pruning. A feasible subset of the final \(A\) must therefore equal \(A\), proving feasibility and optimality. The exclusion of empty feasible objects is essential and explicit.

4. **Recoverable feature lifting: confirmed.** [Section 8d, line 173](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:173).  
   Including the original coordinates makes the feature map injective and permits polynomial membership checking and recovery. Independent feature coefficients provide every linear cost vector required by Theorem F. The inverse problem transfers exactly because its parameters remain those same feature coefficients.

   An optional precision improvement is to say “after a known coordinate permutation, the first \(n\) features are \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\).” The current ordering convention creates no substantive gap.

5. **General-objective remainder: honestly stated.** [Section 8e, lines 177–183](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:177).  
   Normalization supplies a parameter making every feasible target optimal. Attainment and rational encoding are separately assumed, correctly. Global-optimality verification follows from inverse-P, but the available proofs cannot manufacture the penalties, scaling structure, or containment predicates needed to recover an optimum for arbitrary \(f_n\).

   The precise implication is unproved and unrefuted here. Its “OPEN” designation is expressly limited to the dossier. The [2025 public abstract](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/sources/guan-pardalos-zhang-2025.html:681) supports broad unresolved wording, but cannot establish the exact normalized formulation’s literature status through October 2026. The qualifications in [Section 9, lines 187–190](/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/ai4s/inverse-optimization-complexity/proof.md:187) adequately preserve this distinction.

